      */}
      <div class="subsec">
        <p class="eb" data-sommaire>Adoption</p>
        <div class="split">
          <p class="lead">A brand only counts when people use it <em class="em-jaune">without asking.</em></p>
          <div class="body">
            <p>They did - and the proof is what they made with it, not what I drew. The platform was never judged on how it looked. It was judged on whether people reached for the tools instead of improvising around them.</p>
            <p>None of which came from a mandate, because I didn't have one. <strong>So I started where the decisions were made.</strong> Department by department across IT, I worked the leadership teams - and I didn't sell coherence, which nobody wants. I sold what coherence buys them: their people stop spending time on form and spend it on substance, one identity covers uses they'd otherwise fund one by one, and a program gets recognized because it belongs to something, not despite it.</p>
            <p>And I anchored the brand in what those leaders were already accountable for. Their strategy became the vehicle: the identity spoke through their priorities instead of alongside them, so adopting it wasn't extra work - it was how their agenda got heard. <strong>Change management doesn't run beside the strategy. It rides it.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/*
