          <div class="body">
            <p>The first time the ground moved, it was one function. The second time, it was the whole company: in 2022 <strong>Sanofi merged its three brands into one</strong>, and every internal identity was refounded under that single umbrella at once - IT's among them, four years after I had built it. The part nobody had defined was what that brand becomes once it's interactive - so I took it: <strong>translating the new corporate identity through a digital prism</strong>, and shipping the first concepts that proved it could hold on a product, not just on a page.</p>
            <p>I wasn't the only one moving - and by then I'd moved too. The product teams that appeared in the last reset had turned into a function: Sanofi stood up product design in 2021 with the creation of the Consumer Experience team, and I joined it as <strong>Product Design Lead</strong>, carrying four years of brand and communication in with me. So when the rebrand landed, the two forces looking for each other - brand on one side, product on the other - met in a role that had lived in both. <strong>That's the gap I've always worked in.</strong></p>
